[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 746,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 576,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114233,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 576,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Radicular cyst (RC) is an inflammatory odontogenic cyst associated with the root of a non-vital tooth.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 747,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 577,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114234,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 577,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 748,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 578,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114235,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 578,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DA09.8 Radicular cyst</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 749,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 579,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114236,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 579,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> periapical cyst; apical cyst.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> (apical) periodontal cyst; inflammatory dental cyst; dental cyst.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 750,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 580,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114237,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 580,
        "headingText": "<p>Residual cyst (residual radicular cyst)</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-10T16:47:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114238,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RCs are located at the apex of a tooth root, or on the lateral aspect of the root associated with a lateral root canal. The most common site is the anterior maxilla, where 40&ndash;50% arise, followed by the lower molar area {16918602; 21850702}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114239,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RCs are always associated with a non-vital tooth. Patients may report swelling, but RCs are often symptomless and found incidentally on radiological investigation. There may be a history of dental pain or abscess.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>RCs form a round or oval, well-demarcated, corticated radiolucency, usually 10&ndash;20&nbsp;mm in diameter but occasionally larger. Residual cysts are found at a site of previous tooth extraction.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114240,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RC is the most common jaw cyst, accounting for about 60% of all odontogenic cysts. There is a peak incidence in the fourth and fifth decades of life and a slight male predilection {16918602; 21850702}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114241,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RC is caused by chronic inflammation at the apex of a non-vital tooth, after pulp necrosis, usually due to dental caries.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114242,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inflammation stimulates cell rests of Malassez to proliferate and form a cystic cavity that enlarges as a result of osmotic pressure, causing peripheral bone resorption.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114243,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RC is usually a sac-like cystic mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114244,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "<p >RC is lined by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium that is proliferative with a characteristic arcading pattern. Hyaline (Rushton) bodies, mucous (goblet) cells, or small areas of keratinization are not uncommon {27957265; 25859536}. The wall is composed of inflamed fibrous tissue, often with foamy histiocytes. Deposits of cholesterol crystals (clefts) with foreign body giant cells are often seen and may form luminal nodules. Residual and longstanding cysts are less inflamed and have a more regular thin epithelium. Residual cyst (residual radicular cyst) is a radicular cyst that is retained in the jaw after removal of the causative tooth.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-10T16:48:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114245,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "<p>Smears of aspirated cysts may show squamous cells but more commonly show degenerative cyst fluid with cholesterol crystals and debris.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-05T16:18:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 758,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 588,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114246,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 588,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 761,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 591,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114247,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 591,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> non-vital tooth; cyst lined by non-keratinizing epithelium.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 759,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 589,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114248,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 589,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 760,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 590,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114249,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 123,
        "headingId": 590,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Extraction of the causative tooth or root canal treatment remove the cause, and enucleation of the cyst is rarely followed by recurrence {17931389}. Residual cyst may follow extraction alone.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]